Ok, I sense YATVASCIIW (Yet Another Tiles Vs ASCII War) coming up, but
I'll share my opinion anyway. ;-)

I'm no large fan of graphical roguelikes. Especially in Nethack I prefer
pure ASCII, mostly because I don't consider it an adventure game at all
but rather some kind of giant chess board (it isn't about roleplaying at
all (except for the conducts), but learning what actions yield what
results, how different items interact with each other, how to overcome
certain enemies, etc). I've found that colored TTY output is by far the
best way to represent this (I won't have to waste several seconds staring
at the screen, trying to figure out what that 16x16 pixel brown thing in
the upper right corner of the dungeon is - instead, I have a fixed set of
to me already well-known and easily distiguishable characters).

Also, with my prefered computing environment being *nix, nothing beats
ASCII at portability. I can play games on some server way overseas, or I
can lie in bed with my laptop and SSH to my main system (this was actually
the reason I stopped using numpad for movement in favor of hjkl).

I don't even try to imagine the dungeon. When I see a pack of brown "d"
closing in on me, those aren't actually jackals; they're just "d". The
Nethack universe is so perfectly discrete and balanced, that it feels like
trying to overcome the abstraction caused by the ASCII display will just
ruin the experience. The monitor->eye->brain latency is bad enough already.

